Tax Treatment:
Tax deductible under Section 14N of the Income Tax Act 1947 if the expenses do not relate to structural changes for which prior approval from the Commissioner of Building Control is required, and do not relate to:
(a) any designer services or professional services, except designer services or professional services which do not affect the structure of the company's premises with effect from YA 2025;
(b) any antique;
(c) any type of fine art including painting, drawing, print, calligraphy, mosaic, sculpture, pottery or art installation; or
(d) any works carried out to a place of residence provided to or to be provided to employees

The qualifying expenses are capped at $300,000 over a fixed three-year period. The qualifying R&R expenses should not qualify for capital allowance under Section 19 or Section 19A of the Income Tax Act 1947 as these expenses are not incurred for the provision of 'Plant and Machinery.

 Some examples of qualifying expenses are:
(a) general electrical installation and wiring to supply electricity;
(b) general lighting;
(c) hot/ cold water system (pipes, water tanks, etc.);
(d) gas system

Please refer to the IRAS website for more information on the expenditure cap and claim period. Please note that the list of qualifying R&R expenses provided in the IRAS website is not exhaustive.
